  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nRidging across the Intermountain west will persist through \nMonday with northwesterly flow aloft continuing. This flow will \nbriefly transition to west northwesterly flow as a decent upper \nlevel low and trough tracks across southwestern Canada. As this \nfeature tracks east into the arrowhead of Minnesota, ridging \nwill re-amplify across the Rockies, leading to northwesterly \nflow aloft across the forecast area. Cooler temperatures will \nback into the eastern forecast area Tuesday, Wednesday and \nThursday with locations in the west and southwest seeing \nslightly above normal temps. With the persistent northwest and \nwest northwest flow aloft, a steady stream of disturbances \ntracking on the northern periphery of the high, and a \ncontinuation of decent low level moisture across the area, daily\nchances for thunderstorms will continue. The latest NBM \nprobabilistic QPF forecast has good chances 30-50% of measurable\nprecipitation daily Sunday through Thursday. The initialized \nNBM forecast has daily chances of precipitation across the \nforecast area Sunday through Thursday and this seems reasonable \ngiven the degree of low level moisture and favorable flow aloft.\nAs for the severe threat, deep layer shear on the order of 35 \nto 50 KTS persists Sunday through Thursday and will lead to a \ncontinued threat for severe storms across the area. Looking at \nthe deterministic EC and GFS, The upper level pattern will \nremain static across the area into next weekend with persistent \nhigh pressure over the southwestern CONUS and ridging across the\nIntermountain west.",
